In France, voting traditionally involves a low-tech process: voters mark their chosen candidate on a pre-printed paper ballot, which they then place into a ballot box.
After officials have verified the voter's identity, the voter places the ballot into the ballot box and signs the ballot receipt.
French election legislation codifies these processes quite strictly.
Since 1988, it has been required that ballot boxes be transparent, so that observers and voters can confirm at the beginning that no ballots are present and that no additional ballots are added beyond those properly counted and authorized.
Candidates may send representatives to observe all stages of the process. At night, votes are counted under strict supervision by authorized personnel, in accordance with special regulations.
