When a small group of living organisms (a small population) becomes separated from the main population (for example, when they move across a mountain range or river, or migrate to a new island where they cannot easily return), they often find themselves in a different environment than the one they originally came from.
This new environment has different resources and different competitors, so in order for them to become stronger competitors than previously demanded, other features or adaptations are required.
The population remains absolutely unchanged; they are just as in need of adaptation as before.
Over time, the population begins to adapt to its new environment and becomes increasingly less similar to other populations.
Eventually, after thousands, or even millions of years, the two populations become so different in appearance that they can no longer be considered members of the same species.
We call this process speciation, which simply means the formation of new species. Speciation is an inevitable outcome of evolution and a very important part of it.
