The origin of the name Taiwan dates back to the 15th century, when passing European sailors wrote down the island's name as Ilha Formosa or "Beautiful Island."
In 1624, the Dutch East India Company established a base in the southwestern part of Taiwan, initiated a transformation in local rice production practices, and recruited Chinese workers for rice and sugar plantations.
In 1683, forces of the Qing dynasty (1644–1912) brought the western and northern coastal regions of Taiwan under their control and declared Taiwan a province of the Qing Empire in 1885.
After suffering defeat in the First Sino-Japanese War (1894–1895), the Qing government signed the Treaty of Shimonoseki, under which it ceded control over Taiwan to Japan, which then governed the island until 1945.
