Be firm when refusing men, and don't be afraid to stand by your words (regardless of cultural differences, this won't make it any less of a straightforward situation!).
The modern city of Cádiz was founded in the 10th century BCE by Phoenician settlers, and later used as a strategic port by the Phoenicians, Romans, and later the Moors.
The Portuguese rebuilt it under the name of Casca Branca after destroying it, and abandoned it after the 1755 earthquake.
The Sultan of Morocco rebuilt the city as Daru l-Badya and it was named Casablanca by Spanish traders who established trading posts there.
Kasablanka is one of the most interesting places to shop throughout Morocco.
It's easy to find places around Old Medina selling traditional Moroccan products such as clay pots, ceramic vessels, amulets, carpets, and various ornaments, but all of them are for tourists.
Goma is a tourist city located in the far east of the Democratic Republic of the Congo, near Rwanda.
In 2002, the city of Goma, particularly the streets in the city center, was devastated by a lava flow from the Nyiragongo volcano.
Although Goma is sufficiently safe, any visits outside Goma should be carefully studied to understand the ongoing clashes frequently occurring in North Kivu province.
The city is also a base for climbing Mount Nyiragongo, along with the cheapest gorilla trekking in Africa.
You can use a boda-boda (motorcycle taxi) to tour around Goma. For short trips, the regular (local) fare is approximately 500 Congolese francs.
Along with its relatively limited accessibility, "Timbuktu" began to be used as a metaphor for distant, exotic lands.
Today, despite its trade and the presence of an airport, Timbuktu remains a remote village.
Due to the danger posed by desert sands, it was included in the list of World Heritage Sites in danger in 1990.
It was one of the main stops featured in Henry Louis Gates' PBS special series "Africa's Great Civilizations."
This city differs sharply from other cities in the country, as it is more Arab in style than African.
Kruger National Park (KNP) is located in northeastern South Africa, bordering Mozambique to the east, Zimbabwe to the north, and the Limpopo River to the south.
The region covers an area of 19,500 km² and is divided into 14 ecological zones, each supporting different types of wildlife.
It is one of the main attractions in Southern Africa and is regarded as a flagship of South African National Parks (SANParks).
Scientists are currently working on creating a reactor capable of producing energy using the same method.
It may be beneficial to purchase a Wild Card that allows entry to selected parks in South Africa or all national parks in South Africa.
Hong Kong Island has given its name to the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region and is a major destination attracting the attention of many tourists.
The cluster of buildings forming Hong Kong's skyline resembles a shiny, striped pattern due to the presence of Victoria Harbour's waters.
Go to the Kowloon waterfront, located across from the island, to see Hong Kong's best views.
A large part of Hong Kong's urban development has taken place on reclaimed land along the northern coast.
This was once considered the territory of the British colonizers, so if you're looking for evidence of the region's colonial past, this is a great place to start.
The Sundarbans, the world's largest mangrove forest, stretches 80 km (50 miles) from the coastal region into the interior of Bangladesh and India.
Sundarbans was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site. The part of the forest located in India is called "Sundarbans National Park."
However, forests are not just mangrove swamps—they also include the last remaining trees from vast grasslands that once covered the Indian subcontinent.
The Sundarbans cover an area of 3,850 km², with approximately one-third of it being covered by water/wetland areas.
Since 1966, the Sundarbans has been a wildlife sanctuary, and it is estimated that there are currently around 400 royal Bengal tigers and nearly 30,000 spotted deer in this region.
Buses depart throughout the day from the inter-district bus stop on the opposite side of the river, but most of them, especially those heading east and to Jakar/Bumtang, depart between 06:30 and 07:30.
It is recommended to buy tickets several days in advance, as intercity buses are often fully booked.
Many districts are served by Japan's convenient and reliable domestic buses.
Companion taxis are considered fast and convenient ways to travel to places that are not too far away, such as Paro (Nu 150) and Punakha (Nu 200).
The Oyapock River Bridge is a suspension bridge stretching along the Oyapock River, connecting the Brazilian city of Oyapock with the French Guianan town of Saint-Georges-de-l'Oyapock.
The height of the two minor platforms reaches 83 meters and they have two walkways, each 378 meters long and 3.5 meters wide.
The vertical clearance under the bridge is 15 meters. Construction was completed in August 2011, but it remained closed for livestock until March 2017.
The bridge, whose construction of Brazilian border control checkpoints is expected to be completed, is scheduled to be fully operational in September 2017.
The Guarani are the most significant indigenous group currently residing in the eastern region of Paraguay and have traditionally lived as semi-nomadic hunters engaged in subsistence agriculture.
In the Chaco region, local tribes such as the Guaycuru and Payagua lived, sustaining themselves through hunting, fishing, and gathering.
Paraguay, formerly known as the "Great Province of the Indians," emerged in the 16th century as a result of conflicts between Spanish conquerors and local indigenous groups.
The Spanish began their colonial era, which lasted three centuries.
Since its founding in 1537, Paraguay has managed to preserve much of its unique characteristics and distinctiveness.
Argentina is one of the best polo teams in the world and is famous for its players.
In December, the largest tournament of the year will be held at the polo stadiums in Las Cañitas.
You can also see smaller competitions and matches here at other times of the year.
For information on where to purchase tickets for polo matches and the latest news about tournaments, contact the Argentina Polo Association.
The official currency of Folklend is the Folklend pound (FKP), which is fixed in value to one British pound (GBP).
There is a currency exchange facility available at the only bank located on the island opposite the FIC West store in Stenli.
The British pound sterling is generally accepted throughout the islands, and Stanley credit cards and US dollars are also frequently accepted.
Although British and U.S. currencies may be accepted, credit cards might not be accepted in remote islands; find out in advance from the owners which payment methods are accepted.
It is almost impossible to exchange Folklend currency outside the islands, so exchange your money before leaving the islands.
Since Montevideo is located south of the Equator, when it is winter in the Northern Hemisphere, it is summer there, and vice versa.
Montevideo is located in the subtropics; temperatures above +30°C during summer months are common.
It can feel chilly in winter: temperatures rarely drop below the freezing point, but wind and humidity combined can make it feel colder than the thermometer indicates.
There are no distinct "rainy" and "dry" seasons: the amount of rainfall remains almost the same throughout the year.
Although most of the animals in the park are accustomed to seeing people, wild nature is undoubtedly wild, and the animals should not be fed or disturbed.
According to the park authorities, stay at least 100 yards/meters away from bears and wolves, and at least 25 yards/meters away from all other wild animals!
Bison, elk, moose, bears, and nearly all large animals have the ability to attack, no matter how small they may seem.
Every year, dozens of visitors get injured because they fail to maintain a safe distance. These animals can be dangerous, wild, and unpredictable, so please give them their own space.
Also, they attract ants and other wild animals, so avoid carrying or cooking fragrant foods and keep your campsite clean.
Apia is the capital of Samoa. The city is located on the island of Upolu and has fewer than 40,000 inhabitants.
Apia was founded in the 1850s and has been Samoa's official capital since 1959.
When the port was rejected by seven ships from Germany, the USA, and Britain in 1889, the port became a sharp point of maritime conflict.
